# SYNOPSIS

```usage
tail [-N] [file]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`tail` prints the last 10 lines of <file>, or of standard input if there is no <file>.
With `-`<N> it prints the last <N> lines instead; `-0` prints nothing.
A last line with no newline counts as a line, and is printed without one.

`tail` reads the whole input before it prints, keeping only its last 64 KiB, so it never seeks and works the same on a file, a pipe or a terminal.
It does not follow a growing file.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% ns | tail -2
mount tcp!10.0.2.100!5640 /n/host
mount tcp!10.0.2.100!5640 /n/again
```

# SEE ALSO

head(1), sed(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

`tail` says `tail: cannot open it` when it cannot open <file>, and exits `cannot open`.
A malformed `-`<N> prints the usage message and exits `usage`.

# BUGS

Only the last 64 KiB of the input are kept: when the last <N> lines are longer, `tail` prints the last 64 KiB alone, starting part way through a line.

An <N> above 1000009 is refused as a usage error.

Words after <file> are ignored, without a complaint.
A read error ends the input as if it were the end of the file.

@node=posix title="The POSIX command"

In the POSIX namespace, `/lib/ns/posix`, `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `tail` gets sbase's:

```text
tail [-f] [-c num | -m num | -n num | -num] [file ...]
```

It prints the last 10 lines of each <file>, or of standard input, and behaves as POSIX specifies.

: `-n` <num>, `-`<num>
  The last <num> lines; with a leading `+`, from line <num> on.
: `-c` <num>
  The last <num> bytes, or from byte <num> on with a `+`.
: `-m` <num>
  The last <num> characters, UTF-8 decoded, or from character <num> on with a `+`.
: `-f`
  With one <file>, keep printing what is appended to it.
